  "summary": "A distinctive swirling pattern in early thermoset plastics created by mixing resin fillers before curing.",
  "description": "Characterized by dark, swirling patterns in brown, yellow, and black resembling stone or wood grain; resulted from the manufacturing process of mixing phenolic resin with wood flour or other fillers. Popularized as an affordable alternative to traditional materials in consumer goods during the Depression era.",
  "culturalContext": "The reaction between phenol and aldehyde was first reported in 1872 by Adolf von Baeyer, though its use as a commercial product was not considered at the time.\n\nLeo Baekeland was already wealthy due to his invention of Velox photographic paper when he began to investigate the reactions of phenol and formaldehyde in his home laboratory. Chemists had begun to recognize that many natural resins and fibers were polymers. Baekeland's initial intent was to find a replacement for shellac, a material in limited supply because it was made naturally from the secretion of lac insects (specifically Kerria lacca). He produced a soluble phenol-formaldehyde shellac called Novolak, but it was not a market success, even though it is still used to this day (e.g., as a photoresist).",
